2. NON-NEGOTIABLE DATA INTEGRITY RULES

2.1 Single Authoritative Food Catalog

The existing NCP food catalog must remain the sole authoritative catalog used by the calculation engines.

Forbidden:

- Importing USDA records directly into the active catalog.
- Importing EMFID records directly into the active catalog.
- Creating parallel nutritional databases.
- Creating alternate nutrient values for existing foods without a controlled review.
- Automatically replacing existing nutrient values with external values.
- Creating duplicate food records because different sources report different values.
- Changing existing food IDs to accommodate new sources.
- Automatically choosing whichever database provides the highest number of nutrient fields.
- Silently filling missing nutrient values from another database.
- Using AI-generated nutrient values as authoritative analytical data.

External sources may be used only for evidence-based audit and validation.

2.2 Existing Records Must Be Preserved

Unless a specific, verified defect is identified and explicitly approved for remediation:

Preserve:

- Food IDs.
- Arabic and English names.
- Existing aliases and synonyms.
- Food categories.
- Nutrient values.
- Nutrient units.
- Food preparation states.
- Portion definitions.
- Exchange values.
- Calculation bases.
- Provenance.
- Existing food relationships.
- Existing recipe relationships.
- Existing clinical logic.
- Existing data validation rules.

No bulk nutritional corrections.

No mass renaming.

No mass restructuring of the food catalog.

No unnecessary refactoring.

2.3 No Duplicate Food Identity

Before introducing any new food record, the system must assess whether an equivalent food already exists.

Identity assessment must consider:

- Arabic name.
- English name.
- Synonyms.
- Food category.
- Food subtype.
- Preparation method.
- Raw versus cooked status.
- Edible portion.
- Fat content, where relevant.
- Skin, bone, shell, or other relevant characteristics.
- Existing IDs and relationships.

Different nutritional values do not automatically justify creating separate food records.

Food records may legitimately differ when the underlying food identity or preparation state genuinely differs.

Examples:

- Raw rice vs cooked rice.
- Whole milk vs skimmed milk.
- Chicken with skin vs chicken without skin.
- Boiled potato vs fried potato.
- Raw egg vs cooked egg.

The system must distinguish legitimate food variants from duplicate representations.

2.4 Missing Data Must Remain Missing

If a food has no verified sodium value, the calculation engine must not interpret the missing value as zero.

If a food has no verified total sugar value, the calculation engine must not interpret the missing value as zero.

The same principle applies to added sugar.

Missing values must remain explicitly unavailable.

Never fabricate, infer, estimate, or silently substitute a nutrient value.

