PHASE 2 — CLINICAL AND TECHNICAL SPECIFICATION

This phase establishes the exact calculation definitions and reference standards.

No code implementation until the specifications are finalized.

2.1 Population Scope

Current scope:

Healthy adults.

Exclude from this phase:

- Diabetes-specific management.
- Chronic kidney disease.
- Hypertension-specific therapeutic management.
- Cardiovascular disease therapeutic diets.
- Pediatric nutritional requirements.
- Pregnancy and lactation requirements.
- Disease-specific sodium restrictions.
- Disease-specific sugar restrictions.
- Therapeutic nutrition.
- Clinical feeding protocols.
- Disease-specific nutritional prescriptions.

The system may display general population references but must not interpret them as treatment targets.

2.2 Sodium Analysis Specification

The system should calculate the user's recorded or planned sodium intake.

Required calculations:

- Sodium per food item.
- Sodium per portion.
- Sodium per meal.
- Sodium per day.
- Sodium per recall day.
- Sodium per planned diet day.
- Average daily sodium across multiple recorded days, if supported.

Calculation principle:

Sodium from each food must be scaled using the existing food quantity and nutrient calculation infrastructure.

Daily sodium:

Sum all valid sodium contributions from all included foods and recipes.

Units must be consistent and explicitly defined.

Recommended internal standard:

mg sodium per 100 g edible food, where compatible with the existing catalog.

If the current catalog uses another consistent basis, adapt the implementation without creating unnecessary schema changes.

2.3 Sodium and Salt Conversion

If the system displays salt equivalent, use the scientifically correct conversion:

Salt equivalent (g) = Sodium (mg) × 2.5 ÷ 1000

Sodium (mg) = Salt equivalent (g) × 1000 ÷ 2.5

Never confuse sodium with salt.

Never label sodium values as salt values.

The interface must clearly identify units.

2.4 Sodium Reference Values

Before implementation:

Identify appropriate current, authoritative recommendations for healthy adults.

Review sources such as:

- WHO.
- National dietary guidelines.
- Appropriate established nutrition authorities.

Determine whether the interface should display:

- Recommended intake.
- Upper limit or recommended maximum.
- Percentage of the applicable reference.
- Intake status.

Do not combine different authorities' thresholds into one undocumented threshold.

Each displayed reference must include:

- Source.
- Publication or update date, if available.
- Applicable population.
- Definition of the recommendation.
- Units.

If authorities differ, document the difference and select one primary reference for the default interface.

Do not hardcode multiple conflicting thresholds without explaining their purpose.

2.5 Sugar Analysis Specification

The system must distinguish the following whenever supported by verified data:

- Total Sugars.
- Added Sugars.
- Naturally occurring sugars.
- Sugar from individual foods.
- Sugar from recipes.
- Sugar per meal.
- Daily total.
- Average across multiple days.

The system must NOT treat:

- Total Carbohydrate as Total Sugars.
- Naturally occurring sugars as Added Sugars.
- Total Sugars as Added Sugars.
- Missing Added Sugars as zero.

If the current database contains only Total Sugars, the system may calculate Total Sugar Intake.

Added Sugar Analysis must be clearly identified as incomplete whenever the necessary data are unavailable.

2.6 Added Sugar Reference

Evaluate current authoritative recommendations for healthy adults.

Potential sources include:

- WHO.
- Dietary Guidelines for Americans.
- Other recognized nutrition authorities, where relevant.

The system must establish the primary reference before implementation.

If the chosen recommendation is energy-based, calculate the percentage correctly.

Added Sugar % Energy:

(Added Sugar grams × 4 kcal/g) ÷ Total Energy Intake × 100

Use the appropriate denominator and clearly document whether the denominator represents:

- Actual recorded energy intake.
- Planned energy intake.
- Prescribed energy target.

These are not interchangeable.

The system must avoid misleading comparisons when total energy is missing or invalid.

Do not apply adult references to pediatric or other excluded populations.

2.7 Clinical Reference Documentation

Create or update documentation describing:

- Sodium reference.
- Salt conversion.
- Total Sugar definitions.
- Added Sugar definitions.
- Energy conversion.
- Percentage calculations.
- Population scope.
- Data limitations.
- Source citations.
- Calculation assumptions.
- Date of verification.

Clinical reference values must not be embedded in scattered UI components.

Prefer a single documented, validated reference configuration compatible with the existing architecture.

